 { White has a clear material advantage here, and could win in different ways. Gligoric opted for a practical path based on the method of cutting the king off. Chess engines won't support it, but I think that his idea is worthy of attention. } 1. { -KEY- } Rcxf5 $5 Rxf5 2. Rxg4+ Kf6 3. Re4 $1 { This is it - in a similar fashion toExample 194 (Galliamova-Hou Yifan), Black is not allowed to use his king in the defence against the d-pawn. } h5 $2 ( 3... Ra5 $1 { with a side attack was called for, e.g. } 4. Re2 ( 3... h5 3. Kc4 { is well met by } Ra2 { attacking the g2-pawn, e.g } 5. g4 Ra5 6. d5 Ra4 { etc. } ) 
Ra4 $1 { (it's Black's turn to use the method of cutting off the king) } 5. d5 h5 6. Re4 $5 { (an attempt at a bridge) } Ra3 ( 6... Ra8 { also works, but only if Black is ready to meet } 7. Re2 { preparing 8.Kc4 with } Ra4 $1 { again cutting off the enemy king } ) 
7. Ke2 Ra5 8. Rd4 Ke5 { etc. Evidently, a key pattern here is the skewer along the fourth rank which doesn't allow White's king to cross it. } ) 
 ( 3... Rg5 $6 { would also make it, but high accuracy is needed: } 4. Re2 Rg8 $1 5. d5 Rg4 $1 6. d6 Kf7 $1 7. Re7 Kf8 $1 8. Rxh7 Ke8 { . Not a trivial line over the board. } ) 
4. Re2 $1 { Before initiating the advance of the king and d-pawn, White secures the g2-pawn. } h4 5. Kc4 Ra5 6. d5 { Black is short of a good plan, so the rest is easy for Gligoric. } Kf7 7. d6 Ra8 8. Kd5 Ra3 9. d7 Rd3+ 10. Kc6 Rc3+ 11. Kb7 Rb3+ 12. Kc8 Rc3+ 13. Kd8 { Preparing Rf2+ and Rf4 with Lucena's bridge, so Black resigned. } 
