 { Occasionally, it makes sense to cut off the enemy king horizontally from behind; i.e. so that he can't return to the main battlefield. Here is one such case.We have already seen in several examples that in rook and pawn vs. rook endings, the knight's pawn becomes very powerful when the defender's king isn't able to reach its ideal place in front of the pawn. Thus White's strategy is clear here. } 1. { -KEY- } Rh7 $1 { Before advancing the pawn, Bareev cuts the enemy king off along the seventh rank. } ( { a) The immediate } 1. g5 $2 { would only draw after } Re4 $1 2. Kg3 Ke6 ) 
 ( { b) While cutting off the king vertically by } 1. Rf5 $2 { would fail on this occasion due to } Ke6 2. Rf4 ( { or } 2. Kg5 Rg8 { with a frontal attack } ) 
Ke5 $1 { and White can't make progress. } ) 
Ke6 2. Kh5 Rf8 3. g5 Kf5 4. g6 Ke6 5. Kg5 ( 5. g7 { preparing Kg6, was even faster - Bareev. } ) 
Rf1 6. Ra7 ( { A frontal attack with } 6. Rh2 { heading for e2 was also possible. } ) 
Rh1 7. g7 Rg1+ 8. Kh6 Kf6 9. Kh7 Rh1+ 10. Kg8 Rg1 ( { Or } 10... Rb1 11. Ra2 $1 { with a decisive switch of angle; e.g. } Kg6 12. Rg2 Kf6 13. Rh2 { - Bareev. } ) 
11. Kf8 
 1-0

