 { The sixth rank is often used for cutting off the enemy king too, as in the next two examples.White has a slight material advantage, but the knight isn't particularly strong in such a pawn structure, so he should proceed with some care. } 1. { -KEY- } Rc6 $1 { Cutting off Black's king, which means that the queenside pawns become much less of a concern. } Kg6 ( { After } 1... Re7 2. Kd2 Re6 3. Rc7 $1 Re7 4. Rxe7 Kxe7 5. Kd3 Ke6 6. Kd4 { , White should win without major efforts, thanks to his centralized king. } ) 
2. Nc2 g4 ( { Unfortunately for Black, if he advances his queenside pawns these soon turn into targets; e.g. } 2... b5 3. Ra6 $1 { , with Ra5 and Nb4-c6 as a future plan. } ) 
3. Ne3 Rd4 4. g3 $1 { Fixing the enemy kingside pawns as future targets. } Kg5 5. Rc7 Ra4 6. Rg7+ Kh5 7. Nd5 ( { There could follow } 7. Nd5 f5 8. Nf4 Kh6 9. Rg6 Kh7 10. Rf6 Ra5 11. Ke3 Kg7 ( { or } 11. Nd5 Rc5 12. Rf7 ) 
12. Nh5 Kh7 13. Kf4 { , followed by Rxf5, and the knight takes care of the queenside pawns. } ) 

 1-0

