 { This position looks difficult for Black, but Anand defended resourcefully. } 1... { -KEY- } Rc3 $1 { Cutting off the enemy king, while enabling a rear attack on the white e- and f-pawns. } 2. e6 ( { After } 2. Rxh4 Re3 { , the pawn is recovered at once, since } 3. f6 $6 Kg6 { makes no sense for White. } ) 
fxe6 3. fxe6 Kf8 4. Rxh4 Re3 $1 { Initiating a rear attack before the white rook returns to e4. } 5. Rh8+ Kg7 $1 { Forcing the enemy rook to its worst possible angle - in front of the pawn. } ( { After the alternative } 5... Ke7 $2 6. Rh6 { , followed by g2-g3, Kh3 etc, the white king would later be able to reach the g6-square, in contrast to the game. } ) 
6. Re8 Kf6 7. g3 Re4 $1 { Aware that the e-pawn is poisoned (both here and later), Black opts for waiting tactics. } 8. Kg2 Re3 9. Kh3 Re4 10. g4 Re1 { By now it is clear that the unfortunate location of White's rook makes any progress impossible. } 11. Rf8+ Kg7 $1 ( 11... Ke7 $2 { leaves the king fatally cut off after } 12. Rf2 Rxe6 13. Kh4 { (not } 13. g5 $2 Re4 $1 { when Black applies the same idea in horizontal fashion) } Rh6 14. Kg5 Rh8 15. Rf4 { etc. } ) 
12. Re8 Kf6 13. e7 Kg7 $1 14. Kh4 
 1/2-1/2

