 { The idea of cutting off the king along the fifth rank has a specific role in rook vs. pawn endings, which is overlooked surprisingly often in practice. Let's see two examples.Clearly, White is much better, but Black could have transposed into a drawn rook vs. pawn ending, had he proceeded carefully. } 1... { -KEY- } h5 $2 { Even super grandmasters occasionally miss basic endgame methods. } ( { After } 1... Kf6 $1 { , Black holds; e.g. } 2. Kb6 Ra2 3. Rb1 Kg5 4. c6 h5 5. c7 Ra8 ( { but not } 5... Rc2 $2 6. Rb5 $1 { with a decisive bridge on c5 next } ) 
6. Kb7 Rh8 7. c8=Q $11 Rxc8 8. Kxc8 h4 { etc. } ) 
2. Re5 $1 { A key move, which cuts off the enemy king. From now on, any rook vs. pawn ending will be winning. } Kg6 ( { After } 2... Kf6 3. Rxh5 Ke7 4. Kb6 { , both the black king and rook needed to switch flanks, in order to draw by the defence of the long side. } ) 
3. Kb6 Ra8 4. c6 { The rest is easy. } Rb8+ 5. Ka7 Rh8 6. c7 h4 7. Kb7 Kf6 ( { Or } 7... h3 8. c8=Q $11 Rxc8 9. Kxc8 h2 10. Re1 ) 
8. Rc5 ( { After } 8. Rc5 Kg6 9. c8=Q $11 Rxc8 10. Kxc8 $1 { , Black loses the pawn if he advances it, and there is not much else to do. Zhou resigned. } ) 

 1-0

