 { Again the rook vs. pawn ending is around the corner, and both players should prepare carefully for it. } 1. { -KEY- } Kh7 ( { Quite a natural move; but } 1. Rb5 $1 { was a swifter route to victory, applying in advance the method of cutting off the king: } Kc6 2. Rf5 b5 3. Kh7 Kb6 ( { or } 3. Kh7 b4 4. g7 { and, as we already know, Black's king has been fatally sidelined from the battle; } ) 
4. g7 Rd7 5. Kh8 Rxg7 6. Kxg7 Ka5 7. Kf6 Kb4 8. Ke5 Kc4 9. Rf4 Kc3 10. Kd5 { - Atalik. } ) 
Kc6 2. g7 Rd7 3. Rf3 $2 { By now Atalik probably wanted to apply the method that we are investigating, but it no longer works. } ( { a) The obvious } 3. Kh8 $2 { also fails after } Rd8 4. g8=Q $11 Rxg8 5. Kxg8 b5 6. Kf7 Kc5 7. Ke6 Kc4 8. Rb1 b4 9. Ke5 Kc3 { with a quick draw. } ) 
 ( { b) Instead, } 3. Rc3 $1 { was correct, displacing the enemy king before queening. Black has to obstruct his own pawn with } Kb5 ( { since the desired } 3. Rf3 Kd5 { fails to } 4. Rd3 ) 
 { and after } 4. Kh8 Rd8 5. g8=Q $11 Rxg8 6. Kxg8 Ka4 ( { or } 6. Rf3 Kb4 7. Rc8 $1 b5 8. Kf7 Ka3 9. Ke6 b4 10. Kd5 b3 11. Kc4 ) 
7. Kf7 b5 8. Ke6 b4 9. Rc8 $1 b3 10. Kd5 b2 11. Kc4 $1 { White wins - Atalik. } 11. Rb8 { works as well. } ) 
b5 $2 { Returning the favour. } ( { Here } 3... Kc5 $1 { was correct, advancing the king first, as in the previous example. After } 4. Kg6 ( { or } 3... b5 3. Rc3 Kd4 $1 5. Rb3 Kc5 6. Rb1 b5 7. Rc1 Kb4 8. Kh8 Rxg7 9. Kxg7 Ka3 { etc. } ) 
Rxg7 5. Kxg7 b5 6. Kf6 b4 7. Ke5 Kc4 { White's pieces are too badly placed to make victory possible. } ) 
4. Rf5 $1 { Cutting the enemy king off. } b4 ( { After the more tenacious } 4... Kb6 { , White still wins: } 5. Kh8 Rxg7 6. Kxg7 Ka5 7. Kf6 Kb4 8. Ke5 Kc4 9. Ke4 b4 10. Rf8 $1 Kc3 11. Ke3 b3 12. Rc8 { etc - Atalik. } ) 
5. Kh8 ( { In view of } 5. Kh8 Rd8 6. g8=Q $11 Rxg8 7. Kxg8 { Black resigned. } ) 

